{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 5,
  "estimated_minutes": 45,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand flexible premiums, accumulated Policy Value, and the distinction between the COI rate and COI charge before following the challenge to Phoenix’s 2011 rate adjustment."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Insurance meanings of mortality and persistency, investment income versus investment spread, and the underwriting standard drawn from Insurance Law § 4224 require orientation, but the opinion supplies the necessary explanations."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Section 9’s adjacent initial-rate and monthly-adjustment paragraphs must be read with the NAR and Policy Value definitions and the Policy Date and Policy Month definitions."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must distinguish the court’s definitive constructions of permissible pricing factors and unfair discrimination from its unresolved questions about actuarial justification and post-issuance class subdivision."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The Policy Date and Policy Month defects are reciprocal readings of the same paragraphs, while the exhaustive-factor-list issue must be separated from whether Policy Value falls within an enumerated factor."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d06 — Understanding this defect requires distinguishing predicted investment income from an investment spread or net rate of return, then connecting Policy Value to the principal available for investment. The reviewer must also see why allowing Policy Value as an input to investment-earnings expectations does not contradict the court’s exhaustive reading of the six-factor list.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable tracing defined terms and separating related contract theories; specialist actuarial expertise is unnecessary. The main learning task is the universal-life pricing structure, particularly the difference between a rate, the resulting charge, and the funds available for investment. The opinion states most constructions clearly, but targeted rereading is needed to understand why Phoenix can consider Policy Value despite losing its illustrative-list argument. The reviewer should separately record the underwriting standard adopted for unfair discrimination and the court’s express refusal to resolve whether classes could be redefined after issuance."
}
